otifywait","content":"For formats without a built-in parser, pipe through a conversion tool first:"},"637":{"id":"/docs/examples/watch-folder-9","page_id":"/docs/examples/watch-folder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/examples/watch-folder#output-to-jsonl","content":"For streaming ingestion, append to a JSONL file (one JSON object per line):"},"638":{"id":"/docs/examples/watch-folder-10","page_id":"/docs/examples/watch-folder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/examples/watch-folder#sdk-chokidar","content":"For more robust file watching:"},"639":{"id":"/docs/examples/watch-folder-11","page_id":"/docs/examples/watch-folder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/examples/watch-folder#see-also","content":"Process a Directory of Files — batch processing"},"640":{"id":"/docs/examples/watch-folder-12","page_id":"/docs/examples/watch-folder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/examples/watch-folder#see-also","content":"Shell Pipelines & Patterns — more shell patterns"},"641":{"id":"/docs/examples/watch-folder-13","page_id":"/docs/examples/watch-folder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/examples/watch-folder#see-also","content":"simple — single-shot extraction"},"642":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","type":"page","content":"Chunking & Token Budgets","breadcrumbs":["Struktur","Concepts"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/chunking"},"643":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-0","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking","content":"How large documents are split and merged to fit within context windows."},"644":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-1","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#the-context-window-problem","content":"The context window problem"},"645":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-2","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#how-splitting-works","content":"How splitting works"},"646":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-3","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#images-and-maximages","content":"Images and `maxImages`"},"647":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-4","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#why-simple-does-not-chunk","content":"Why `simple` does not chunk"},"648":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-5","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#merging-partial-results","content":"Merging partial results"},"649":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-6","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#llm-merge-parallel-doublepass","content":"LLM merge (parallel, doublePass)"},"650":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-7","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#schema-aware-auto-merge","content":"Schema-aware auto-merge"},"651":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-8","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#deduplication","content":"Deduplication"},"652":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-9","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#when-dedup-matters","content":"When dedup matters"},"653":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-10","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#see-also","content":"See also"},"654":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-11","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#the-context-window-problem","content":"LLMs have finite context windows. Documents — especially multi-page PDFs, large datasets, or many files at once — often exceed them. Struktur's chunker splits artifact contents into batches that fit within a configurable token budget (`chunkSize`)."},"655":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-12","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#how-splitting-works","content":"Splitting happens at two levels:"},"656":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-13","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#how-splitting-works","content":"**ArtifactSplitter:** splits a single large artifact's contents into smaller parts, respecting content slice boundaries (e.g., page boundaries)."},"657":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-14","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#how-splitting-works","content":"**ArtifactBatcher:** groups artifacts or artifact parts into batches that stay within the token budget and optional image count limit."},"658":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-15","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#how-splitting-works","content":"The tokenizer uses a character-approximation (not exact token counting). `chunkSize` defaults to 10,000 tokens."},"659":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-16","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#images-and-maximages","content":"Some strategies accept `maxImages` to cap how many images appear per chunk. This matters when images consume disproportionate context. If a batch would exceed `maxImages`, extra images are moved to the next batch."},"660":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-17","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#why-simple-does-not-chunk","content":"The `simple` strategy loads all artifacts as-is. If the input exceeds the context window, the LLM call may fail or produce degraded results. For large inputs, use a chunked strategy."},"661":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-18","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#why-simple-does-not-chunk","content":"This is a deliberate trade-off: `simple` is fast and cheap for small inputs, not suitable for large ones."},"662":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-19","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#merging-partial-results","content":"When a document is split into N chunks and each chunk is extracted independently, you get N partial objects."},"663":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-20","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#merging-partial-results","content":"For a scalar-heavy schema (title, author, date), you want the best answer from all chunks."},"664":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-21","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#merging-partial-results","content":"For an array-heavy schema (line items, product listings), you want to concatenate all arrays and remove duplicates."},"665":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-22","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#llm-merge-parallel-doublepass","content":"These strategies send all partial results to a **merge model** in a single call, with a prompt asking it to produce a single coherent output. The merge model sees the full schema and all partial outputs."},"666":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-23","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#llm-merge-parallel-doublepass","content":"This is powerful but costs extra tokens."},"667":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-24","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#schema-aware-auto-merge","content":"`parallelAutoMerge`, `sequentialAutoMerge`, and `doublePassAutoMerge` use `SmartDataMerger`:"},"668":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-25","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#schema-aware-auto-merge","content":"**Arrays:** concatenated. `items` from chunk 1 + `items` from chunk 2 = `items` in merged."},"669":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-26","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#schema-aware-auto-merge","content":"**Objects:** shallow-merged. Keys from later chunks overwrite keys from earlier ones."},"670":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-27","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#schema-aware-auto-merge","content":"**Scalars:** prefer newer non-empty values."},"671":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-28","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#schema-aware-auto-merge","content":"This approach avoids an extra LLM call and works well for list-extraction schemas. It does not handle complex cross-chunk synthesis — for that, use LLM merge."},"672":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-29","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#deduplication","content":"After auto-merging concatenated arrays, there may be duplicates. Dedup runs in two stages:"},"673":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-30","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#deduplication","content":"**Stage 1: CRC32 hash-based.** Exact duplicates (byte-for-byte identical after stable JSON stringification) are removed without any LLM call. Fast and cheap."},"674":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-31","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#deduplication","content":"**Stage 2: LLM-based semantic dedup.** A dedupe model is given the merged array and asked to identify semantically equivalent entries (e.g., \"iPhone 15\" vs \"Apple iPhone 15 128GB\"). It returns a list of dot-path keys to remove (e.g., `items.3`)."},"675":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-32","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#deduplication","content":"Only the auto-merge variants include this step."},"676":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-33","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#when-dedup-matters","content":"Dedup is valuable when:"},"677":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-34","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#when-dedup-matters","content":"The same item legitimately appears in multiple chunks."},"678":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-35","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#when-dedup-matters","content":"The same document segment appears in multiple artifacts."},"679":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-36","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#when-dedup-matters","content":"Dedup adds token cost and latency. For schemas without arrays, or for inputs with no expected overlap, use strategies without auto-merge."},"680":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-37","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#see-also","content":"The Extraction Pipeline — the full flow"},"681":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-38","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#see-also","content":"Strategies — which strategies use chunking"},"682":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-39","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#see-also","content":"parallel — chunked parallel extraction"},"683":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-40","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#see-also","content":"sequential — chunked sequential extraction"},"684":{"id":"/docs/explanation","page_id":"/docs/explanation","type":"page","content":"Explanation","breadcrumbs":["Struktur","Concepts"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation"},"685":{"id":"/docs/explanation-0","page_id":"/docs/explanation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation#topics","content":"Topics"},"686":{"id":"/docs/explanation-1","page_id":"/docs/explanation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation","content":"Deep dives into Struktur's architecture, strategies, and design decisions."},"687":{"id":"/docs/explanation-2","page_id":"/docs/explanation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation#topics","content":"pipeline — How data flows through Struktur"},"688":{"id":"/docs/explanation-3","page_id":"/docs/explanation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation#topics","content":"chunking — Token-aware document splitting"},"689":{"id":"/docs/explanation-4","page_id":"/docs/explanation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation#topics","content":"validation — Schema validation and retry logic"},"690":{"id":"/docs/explanation-5","page_id":"/docs/explanation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation#topics","content":"strategies — Extraction strategies overview"},"691":{"id":"/docs/explanation-6","page_id":"/docs/explanation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation#topics","content":"preprocessing — Input handling and artifact format"},"692":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","type":"page","content":"Parsers","breadcrumbs":["Struktur","Concepts"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/parsers"},"693":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-0","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers","content":"How Struktur converts files into Artifacts before extraction."},"694":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-1","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#mime-detection","content":"MIME Detection"},"695":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-2","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#built-in-parsers","content":"Built-in Parsers"},"696":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-3","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#custom-parsers","content":"Custom Parsers"},"697":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-4","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#npm-package-parser","content":"npm package parser"},"698":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-5","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#shell-command-file-based","content":"Shell command (file-based)"},"699":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-6","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#shell-command-stdin-based","content":"Shell command (stdin-based)"},"700":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-7","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#inline-parser-sdk-only","content":"Inline parser (SDK only)"},"701":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-8","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#parser-resolution-order","content":"Parser Resolution Order"},"702":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-9","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#ad-hoc-parser-override","content":"Ad-hoc Parser Override"},"703":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-10","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#sdk-usage","content":"SDK Usage"},"704":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-11","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#see-also","content":"See also"},"705":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-12","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers","content":"Struktur's parser system converts files into Artifact format before any LLM work happens. Parsers are resolved by MIME type and are fully configurable."},"706":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-13","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#mime-detection","content":"MIME type is detected in three layers (tried in order):"},"707":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-14","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#mime-detection","content":"**Magic bytes** (authoritative): PDF (`%PDF-`), PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP, and ZIP-based Office formats are identified from the first bytes of the file."},"708":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-15","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#mime-detection","content":"**npm `detectFileType` callback**: A custom npm parser may export a `detectFileType(header: Uint8Array): boolean` function to claim MIME types beyond what magic bytes cover."},"709":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-16","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#mime-detection","content":"**File extension database**: Fallback for inputs where magic bytes don't match a known signature."},"710":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-17","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#mime-detection","content":"Override MIME detection with `--mime <type>` on any command that accepts input."},"711":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-18","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#built-in-parsers","content":"MIME type"},"712":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-19","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#built-in-parsers","content":"Behavior"},"713":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-20","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#built-in-parsers","content":"`application/pdf`"},"714":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-21","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#built-in-parsers","content":"Per-page text via `pdf-parse`. Embedded images require `--images`. Page screenshots require `--screenshots`. Image deduplication filters images smaller than \\~80px. Non-fatal on image/screenshot failures."},"715":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-22","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#built-in-parsers","content":"`text/*`"},"716":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-23","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#built-in-parsers","content":"Split on double newlines into content slices."},"717":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-24","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#built-in-parsers","content":"`image/*`"},"718":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-25","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#built-in-parsers","content":"Single-content artifact with one media item."},"719":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-26","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#built-in-parsers","content":"`application/json`"},"720":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-27","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#built-in-parsers","content":"If it validates as `SerializedArtifact[]`, passed through unchanged without invoking any parser."},"721":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-28","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#custom-parsers","content":"Four types are supported. Three are registered with `struktur config parsers add`, and one is for SDK-only use."},"722":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-29","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#npm-package-parser","content":"Install a package that implements the `NpmParserModule` interface:"},"723":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-30","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#npm-package-parser","content":"When both `parseFile` and `parseStream` are exported, Struktur prefers `parseFile` for file inputs (zero-copy) and `parseStream` for buffer or stdin inputs. A temp file is created as a fallback if needed."},"724":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-31","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#npm-package-parser","content":"Register it:"},"725":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-32","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#shell-command-file-based","content":"The `FILE_PATH` placeholder is replaced with the actual file path at runtime. For buffer inputs, a temp file is created automatically."},"726":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-33","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#shell-command-file-based","content":"`FILE_PATH` must appear in the command string — an error is thrown if it is missing."},"727":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-34","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#shell-command-file-based","content":"The command must write `SerializedArtifact[]` JSON to stdout."},"728":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-35","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#shell-command-stdin-based","content":"File contents are piped to the command's stdin."},"729":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-36","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#shell-command-stdin-based","content":"The command must write `SerializedArtifact[]` JSON to stdout. Plain text output will fail validation."},"730":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-37","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#inline-parser-sdk-only","content":"For code-only parsers in the SDK, use `InlineParserDef`:"},"731":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-38","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#inline-parser-sdk-only","content":"Inline parsers are the modern replacement for the deprecated `providers` registry."},"732":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-39","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#parser-resolution-order","content":"For any input, parsers are resolved in this order:"},"733":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-40","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#parser-resolution-order","content":"`--parser <pkg>` flag on the CLI — always wins, bypasses all config"},"734":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-41","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#parser-resolution-order","content":"Parser configured for the detected MIME type (`config parsers add`)"},"735":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-42","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#parser-resolution-order","content":"Built-in parser (PDF, text/\\*, image/\\*, JSON)"},"736":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-43","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#parser-resolution-order","content":"Error with a suggestion to use `config parsers add`"},"737":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-44","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#ad-hoc-parser-override","content":"Use `--parser` to override the configured parser for a single run without changing config:"},"738":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-45","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#sdk-usage","content":"Pass a `parserConfig` to `parse` to use custom parsers without CLI config:"},"739":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-46","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#see-also","content":"config parsers — CLI commands for managing parsers"},"740":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-47","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#see-also","content":"parse — Convert files to artifact JSON"},"741":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-48","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#see-also","content":"Artifact Format — The output data structure"},"742":{"id":"/docs/explanation/parsers-49","page_id":"/docs/explanation/parsers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/parsers#see-also","content":"Built-in Input Types — SDK input helpers"},"743":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","type":"page","content":"Extraction Lifecycle","breadcrumbs":["Struktur","Concepts"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline"},"744":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-0","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","content":"How data flows through Struktur from input to output."},"745":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-1","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#inputs-and-artifacts","content":"Inputs and Artifacts"},"746":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-2","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#the-strategy-layer","content":"The Strategy layer"},"747":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-3","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#validation-inside-the-loop","content":"Validation inside the loop"},"748":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-4","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#the-result","content":"The result"},"749":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-5","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#see-also","content":"See also"},"750":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-6","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#inputs-and-artifacts","content":"Struktur converts input files into **Artifacts** before extraction. For plain text or stdin, this is trivial. For structured files (PDFs, Office documents), Struktur runs a parser — built-in or custom — that extracts text and images per-page."},"751":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-7","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#inputs-and-artifacts","content":"See Parsers for how files are converted to artifacts.\nSee Artifact Format for the data structure."},"752":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-8","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#the-strategy-layer","content":"A strategy is the orchestration engine. It decides how to split the input, how many LLM calls to make, whether to run them concurrently or sequentially, and how to combine results."},"753":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-9","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#the-strategy-layer","content":"Built-in strategies cover the common patterns. You can also write your own."},"754":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-10","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#the-strategy-layer","content":"See Strategies for the strategy concept."},"755":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-11","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#validation-inside-the-loop","content":"The validation loop is a key differentiator. Every LLM response is validated against the schema using Ajv **before** the strategy considers it done. If validation fails, the errors are serialized and sent back to the model as a follow-up message."},"756":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-12","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#validation-inside-the-loop","content":"**Smart validation**: For multi-step strategies (parallel, sequential, double-pass), Struktur uses lenient validation during intermediate steps—required field violations are allowed until the final step. This prevents false failures when data is split across chunks. Use the `strict` option to disable this behavior."},"757":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-13","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#validation-inside-the-loop","content":"Most extractions converge within two attempts. This happens **inside** the strategy, not as a post-processing step."},"758":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-14","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#validation-inside-the-loop","content":"Default: `maxAttempts` = 3."},"759":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-15","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#validation-inside-the-loop","content":"See Validation & Retries for the validation concept."},"760":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-16","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#the-result","content":"The extraction result has three fields:"},"761":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-17","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#the-result","content":"Field"},"762":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-18","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#the-result","content":"Description"},"763":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-19","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#the-result","content":"`data`"},"764":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-20","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#the-result","content":"Validated output matching your schema. If `error` is set, may not be trustworthy."},"765":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-21","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#the-result","content":"`usage`"},"766":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-22","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#the-result","content":"Aggregated token counts across all LLM calls."},"767":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-23","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#the-result","content":"`error`"},"768":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-24","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#the-result","content":"Set if extraction encountered a non-fatal error."},"769":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-25","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#see-also","content":"Parsers — how files are converted to artifacts"},"770":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-26","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#see-also","content":"Artifacts — the input format"},"771":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-27","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#see-also","content":"Strategies — orchestration patterns"},"772":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-28","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#see-also","content":"Chunking & Token Budgets — how large documents are split"},"773":{"id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline-29","page_id":"/docs/explanation/pipeline","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/pipeline#see-also","content":"Validation & Retries — the retry loop"},"774":{"id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format","page_id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format","type":"page","content":"Artifact Format","breadcrumbs":["Struktur","Concepts","Document Processing"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format"},"775":{"id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format-0","page_id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format","content":"The artifact abstraction and complete specification."},"776":{"id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format-1","page_id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format#the-normalization-boundary","content":"The normalization boundary"},"777":{"id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format-2","page_id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format#what-an-artifact-contains","content":"What an artifact contains"},"778":{"id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format-3","page_id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format#why-text--images-together","content":"Why text + images together?"},"779":{"id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format-4","page_id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format#complete-specification","content":"Complete specification"},"780":{"id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format-5","page_id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format#top-level-shape","content":"Top-level shape"},"781":{"id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format-6","page_id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format#content-slices","content":"Content slices"},"782":{"id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format-7","page_id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format#images","content":"Images"},"783":{"id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format-8","page_id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format#complete-example","content":"Complete example"},"784":{"id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format-9","page_id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format#validation","content":"Validation"},"785":{"id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format-10","page_id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format#built-in-artifact-creation","content":"Built-in artifact creation"},"786":{"id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format-11","page_id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format#see-also","content":"See also"},"787":{"id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format-12","page_id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format#the-normalization-boundary","content":"Different document types (PDF, HTML, Excel, email) require different parsing strategies. But LLM extraction is the same regardless of source format. The Artifact is the normalized form that crosses that boundary."},"788":{"id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format-13","page_id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format#the-normalization-boundary","content":"Struktur only cares about what is in the artifact, not where it came from."},"789":{"id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format-14","page_id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format#what-an-artifact-contains","content":"An artifact has:"},"790":{"id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format-15","page_id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format#what-an-artifact-contains","content":"`id`: unique identifier"},"791":{"id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format-16","page_id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format#what-an-artifact-contains","content":"`type`: type hint (`text`, `image`, `pdf`, `file`)"},"792":{"id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format-17","page_id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format#what-an-artifact-contains","content":"`contents`: a sequence of content slices"},"793":{"id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format-18","page_id":"/docs/explanation/preprocessing/artifact-format","tags":[],"